Nike shows investors its new plan on the seventeenth of **November**.

// CARD a6-06 · SEE: MOTIF: the two shoes on the track; on "question" NIKE edges forward, ADIDAS level; CAN NIKE WIN BACK THE SHELF? · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@lane_race;bg:nike_vs_adidas_art_running_track;a:nva_cut_sneaker_red;b:nva_cut_sneaker_blue;alab:NIKE;blab:ADIDAS;ax:0.56;bx:0.56;go:question;lab:CAN NIKE WIN BACK THE SHELF?;out:right"
{awe} The question is whether a company can win back a shelf it walked away **from**.

// CARD a6-07 · SEE: the real Times Square crowd under billboards, the camera travelling along it · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@reel;clip:stock_city_newyork_street_09;move:right;z:.2;in:right"
So remember that shelf from nineteen eighty-five, because Nike built an empire on being everywhere and then decided it didn't need to be, while Adidas simply showed up wherever people **shop**.
If Nike gets its shoes back on those shelves, this was a bad two years, and if it doesn't, the brothers' old rival may be the one still **standing**.

